# Technical Analysis

A JWT normally consists of three components:

```text
HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE
```

The signature provides integrity protection for the header and payload.

In this lab, the original token used:

```json
"alg": "RS256"
```

After manipulation, the header specified:

```json
"alg": "none"
```

This resulted in:

```text
HEADER.PAYLOAD.
```

The application failed to reject the unsigned token and instead trusted the claims contained within it.

The attack can therefore be represented as:

```text
Legitimate JWT
      │
      ▼
Modify "alg"
RS256 ─────────► none
      │
      ▼
Modify / retain claims
sub = administrator
      │
      ▼
Remove signature
      │
      ▼
Forged JWT
      │
      ▼
Send as session cookie
      │
      ▼
Application accepts token
      │
      ▼
Administrator privileges
```

